{"name":"Magnetic Green","hex":"#2b6867","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"magnetic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, operating by, or caused by magnetism.","Having the properties of a magnet, especially the ability to draw or pull."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magnetic"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Magnetism","blocks":[{"text":"Magnetism is the class of physical attributes that occur through a magnetic field, which allows objects to attract or repel each other. Because both electric currents and magnetic moments of elementary particles give rise to a magnetic field, magnetism is one of two aspects of electromagnetism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Metal_shavings_around_bar_magnet_%28close-up%29.jpg/330px-Metal_shavings_around_bar_magnet_%28close-up%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetism","wikidata":"Q3294789"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Lodestone_attracting_nails.png/500px-Lodestone_attracting_nails.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Lodestone_attracting_nails.png/500px-Lodestone_attracting_nails.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lodestone, a natural magnet, attracting iron nails. Ancient humans discovered the property of magnetism from lodestone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALodestone_attracting_nails.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg/500px-Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg/1280px-Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg/500px-Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An illustration from Gilbert's 1600 De Magnete showing one of the earliest methods of making a magnet. A blacksmith holds a piece of red-hot iron in a north–south direction and hammers it as it cools. The magnetic field of the Earth aligns the domains, leaving the iron a weak magnet.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/A_man_is_violently_rubbed_with_magnets._Coloured_lithograph_Wellcome_V0011767.jpg/500px-A_man_is_violently_rubbed_with_magnets._Coloured_lithograph_Wellcome_V0011767.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/A_man_is_violently_rubbed_with_magnets._Coloured_lithograph_Wellcome_V0011767.jpg/1280px-A_man_is_violently_rubbed_with_magnets._Coloured_lithograph_Wellcome_V0011767.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/A_man_is_violently_rubbed_with_magnets._Coloured_lithograph_Wellcome_V0011767.jpg/500px-A_man_is_violently_rubbed_with_magnets._Coloured_lithograph_Wellcome_V0011767.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drawing of a medical treatment using magnetic brushes. Charles Jacque 1843, France.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_man_is_violently_rubbed_with_magnets._Coloured_lithograph_Wellcome_V0011767.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Electromagnet.gif/500px-Electromagnet.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Electromagnet.gif/500px-Electromagnet.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An electromagnet attracts paper clips when current is applied, creating a magnetic field. The electromagnet loses them when current